    "why": {
      "FLAT.3": "The evidence provided, including the patient's demographics, chief complaint, and histopathology results, strongly supports the diagnosis of apocrine gland carcinoma, which is the label for the FLAT.3 champion. The presence of GATA3-positive and estrogen receptor-positive apocrine glands in close association with diffuse tumor cell infiltration, as well as the positive immunohistochemistry results for pancytokeratin, cytokeratin 20, and GCDFP15, are all consistent with this diagnosis."
    }
  },
  "1322f6f9bd37de201b9204935af4459997f83ae081e832cca181303c836e7e4e": {
    "fact_rationales": {
      "F11": "The presence of GATA3-positive and estrogen receptor–positive apocrine glands in close association to diffuse tumor cell infiltration supports a diagnosis of apocrine gland carcinoma.",
      "F12": "Isolated tumor cells in a sentinel lymph node may indicate metastasis, but the significance of this finding depends on the primary diagnosis.",
      "F8": "Tumor cells in an 'Indian file pattern' are more typical of certain carcinomas, but not exclusive to them.",
      "F9": "Positive immunohistochemistry for pancytokeratin, cytokeratin 20, GCDFP15, estrogen receptor, and GATA3 antibodies is highly suggestive of apocrine gland carcinoma."
